{
	title: 'Terminal web links carry a one-time code',
	description: "The host knows its public web address: a webUrl setting in config.ason (settings table), empty by default meaning http://localhost:<webPort>. Every web link the terminal prints points there: image markers (/image/<name>), paste markers, each tab label in the tab bar (its number links to <webUrl>/<session id>, questions.md item 47) and any later link to a block. A link's hidden OSC 8 target carries ?auth=<code>, a one-time code of task 8a; its visible text never does (questions.md item 26: codes appear in view only as the reply to /auth or ./run auth). Codes are not minted per frame: the host gives each terminal client (host terminal or peer, over its connection) a link code and replaces it once it is redeemed and before it is 5 minutes old, so a link on screen keeps working for at least 5 of a code's 10 minutes; once one click has logged a browser in, its cookie covers every later link. Link codes are never written to history, drafts or logs. A web session token lasts 10 years, as task 8a now says (it was built with 30 days). Any page the host serves (/, /<id>, /image/<name>, /paste/<name>) asked for without a valid code or cookie shows the login gate, not a bare 401, and after a right code lands on the page that was asked for. Tests: a printed link's target holds a code the host accepts and its visible text none; a used or expired code on /image/<name> gets the gate, which returns there after login; the code is replaced after redemption; no code reaches any history file.",
	status: 'planned',
	needs: [],
	foldInto: ['8a', 'qy', '7w']
}
